Create a view class that inherits **`MethodView`** and implement a `get` method for the GET request and a `post` method for the POST request. Then register the class with `app.add_url_rule` using its `as_view` helper. Flask will dispatch the request to the method whose name matches the HTTP verb.

```python
from flask.views import MethodView
from flask import Flask, request, redirect, url_for, session

app = Flask(__name__)

class CounterAPI(MethodView):
    def get(self):
        return str(session.get("counter", 0))

    def post(self):
        session["counter"] = session.get("counter", 0) + 1
        return redirect(url_for("counter"))

app.add_url_rule("/counter", view_func=CounterAPI.as_view("counter"))
```

* `MethodView` “dispatches request methods to the corresponding instance methods… each HTTP method maps to a method of the class with the same (lowercase) name” [5].
* The documentation notes that “For APIs it can be helpful to use a different function for each HTTP method. `MethodView` extends the basic `View` to dispatch to different methods of the class based on the request method” [1].

With this setup, a GET to `/counter` calls `CounterAPI.get`, while a POST to the same URL calls `CounterAPI.post`.
